There are a whole bunch of ways to resume a stopped (suspended) job.
When I am editing code with vi(m) I often have a half dozen or a dozen
or even more editing jobs in the same shell window, all but the
current one stopped. You get a list with 'jobs'. You resume the
current one (most recently stopped) by typing 'fg' or '%+', or the
next most recently stopped with '%-', or '%3' to resume whatever job
#3 is (per the numbering from 'jobs'), or so on.

In theory you can use stty to enable/disable ^Z (for suspend) but this
may or may not work depending on your circumstances.



